Allow archiving workflow history to Parquet files stored in a pluggable backend (supports S3, GCS through its S3-compatible API, and local files; Azure isn't supported yet). The archiver writes with `hyparquet-writer` and reads with DuckDB (`@duckdb/node-api`), both optional peer dependencies of `@dbos-inc/dbos-enterprise`, along with `@aws-sdk/client-s3` and `@aws-sdk/lib-storage` for S3 and GCS. It exposes this configuration:

```typescript
new ParquetArchive(uri: string, options?: {
  s3Client?: S3ClientLike;
  duckdbSecret?: Record<string, DuckDBValue>;
  duckdbConfig?: Record<string, DuckDBValue>;
  archiveID?: string; // Defaults to the URI
  targetFileBytes?: number; // Defaults to 128 MiB
  maxFlushIntervalMs?: number; // Defaults to 10 minutes
  maxFlushWorkflows?: number; // Defaults to 100,000
  serializer?: DBOSSerializer;
  allowExtensionDownload?: boolean;
  extensionDirectory?: string;
  queryTimeoutMs?: number;
  activeArchiver?: boolean; // Defaults to true
})
```

Archives are fully queryable. For performance (because the archive is partitioned by workflow completion time), all point operations on workflows require the workflow's timestamp.

```typescript
class ParquetArchive {
  /** The archived status of one workflow at one completion, or null if it isn't archived. */
  getWorkflowStatus(
    workflowID: string,
    completedAt: number,
    options?: { loadInput?: boolean; loadOutput?: boolean },
  ): Promise<WorkflowStatus | null>;

  /** The steps of one workflow at one completion, by function ID. */
  listWorkflowSteps(
    workflowID: string,
    completedAt: number,
    options?: { loadOutput?: boolean; limit?: number; offset?: number },
  ): Promise<StepInfo[]>;

  /** The events one workflow had set at one completion, by key. */
  getEvents(workflowID: string, completedAt: number): Promise<Record<string, unknown>>;

  /** The messages one workflow had been sent at one completion, oldest first. */
  getNotifications(workflowID: string, completedAt: number): Promise<ArchivedNotification[]>;

  /** The values one workflow had written to a stream at one completion, in order. */
  readStream<T>(workflowID: string, completedAt: number, key: string): AsyncGenerator<T, void, unknown>;

  /**
   * A page of archived workflows, filtered as DBOS.listWorkflows filters live ones.
   *
   * The archive is read newest first, a page at a time, so limit is required. Each
   * archived snapshot is filtered on its own, and of a workflow's matching snapshots
   * the latest is returned, newest created first.
   */
  listWorkflows(input: Omit<GetWorkflowsInput, 'limit'> & { limit: number }): Promise<WorkflowStatus[]>;
}
```

It is also possible to rehydrate archived workflows, from the app or from a client given the archive:

```typescript
/** Copy one archived snapshot of a workflow back into the system database. */
DBOS.importArchivedWorkflow<T = unknown>(
  workflowID: string,
  completedAt: number,
): Promise<WorkflowHandle<Awaited<T>>>;

(await DBOSClient.create({ ..., workflowArchive: archive })).importArchivedWorkflow<T = unknown>(
  workflowID: string,
  completedAt: number,
): Promise<WorkflowHandle<Awaited<T>>>;
```

Conductor reads the archive through new messages: `archive_list_workflows`, `archive_get_workflow`, `archive_list_steps`, `archive_get_workflow_events`, `archive_get_workflow_notifications`, `archive_get_workflow_streams` and `import_archived_workflow`. Executor info reports `workflow_archive` so Conductor knows which apps have one.

The way it works is that a leader process (chosen using advisory locks) periodically archives recently terminated workflows by exporting them in batches into Parquet files in the chosen backend partitioned by workflow completion time. The archive format will be fully documented; it supports external query engines (DuckDB, Athena, etc.). Exports are a batch read from Postgres followed by a batch write to the chosen store, so the performance overhead of workflow archiving is negligible compared to the cost of workflow processing.

